WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.779
Thanks to Brilliant for supporting this episode
of Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:17.150
Go to Brilliant.org/microcosmos to get 20%
off an annual premium subscription!

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:29.739
We’re nearing the end of our fourth season
of Journey to the Microcosmos, and over that

00:00:29.739 --> 00:00:35.110
time, we’ve been able to see microbes through
all kinds of lenses—literally—thanks to

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:37.320
James, our master of microscopes.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:42.690
For the rest of us on the Microcosmos team,
it’s been really cool to not only see the

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.620
footage that James has recorded, but also
see the journey that he has been on with the

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.080
microscopes themselves.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.870
Now, we’ve been able to make this journey
because of all of you and the support you

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:59.010
have shown to this channel by watching and
commenting and sharing with others, or even

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.870
and especially by supporting us on Patreon.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:06.510
We’ve all been on this journey together,
so let’s look back on where it’s taken

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:14.420
us, and also get a peek at where we’re going
in the future.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:20.670
In the first season, James started out with
two microscopes.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:26.630
One was a microscope that he assembled himself
from different parts for under $200.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:31.850
The other microscope was a Motic BA310 that
he received from a microscope company shortly

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:33.690
after we started this channel.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:39.420
And these microscopes give us so much beautiful
footage, thanks in part to the different techniques

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:45.850
that James used with them, whether that was
bright-field or phase contrast or darkfield,

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:51.770
or some other technique to manipulate the
light shining on the microcosmos.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:56.810
But these microscopes also had their limits,
so we were really excited to see what would

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.961
happen when James bought a new microscope
before the start of our third season: the

00:02:01.961 --> 00:02:04.570
Zeiss Axioscope 5.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.640
This new microscope came with higher quality
objectives (which are the lenses on the bottom

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.840
of the microscope), as well as prisms that
made it possible for us to look at microbes

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:20.190
with differential interference contrast microscopy,
a technique that sharpened their features

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:24.090
and made them seem more three-dimensional
on our screen.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:28.840
We have videos exploring these different techniques
and microscopes, so if you want to learn more

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:36.080
about them, we recommend checking those out
because light is much weirder than it seems.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.870
But we wanted to do a quick summary for reasons
that are probably very clear from the title

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.800
of this video: we have upgraded our microscope!

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:51.510
The Zeiss Axioscope 5 was a very exciting
change, but it was also an expensive one.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:56.930
So when James bought it, he was only able
to get two objectives: a 20x objective and

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.190
a 63x objective.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:05.190
Adding that to the 10x magnification the eyepiece
gives, let us magnify our samples by 200x

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:09.710
and 630x respectively, which is not bad.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:15.831
But a standard microscope usually comes with
5-6 objectives for a reason: the microcosmos

00:03:15.831 --> 00:03:21.400
is full of things both small and large to
observe and there isn’t a one-size-fits-all

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.610
objective that you can use to observe everything.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:29.760
So one of James’ priorities was expanding
his set of objectives so that he could have

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.180
the flexibility to see the microcosmos at
more scales.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:40.280
And thanks to his recent upgrade, he’s now
gone from a set of 2 to a set of six that

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:51.510
range from 5x to 100x and that gives us a
magnification range from 50x to 1000x.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:56.380
Since I have gotten my own prototype of the
microcosmos microscope that we are manufacturing

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:01.739
thanks to backers of our kickstarter, I’ve
been amazed at how important it is to have

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:07.310
this variety of options, and how much time
I spend on the lower magnifications and well

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:11.480
as going deeper .
So without further ado, let’s introduce

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:16.290
these different objectives by looking at a
ciliate called Tetrahymena that’s taken

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:27.810
up residence in a rotifer exoskeleton.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:34.240
With the 5x objective, we can see little dots
swimming in what used to be empty space, but

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.110
it’s hard to make out the details.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:41.580
When we move to the 10x objective, we can
start to make out shapes of the Tetrahymena

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:47.190
and more of the emptiness around them, but
it’s difficult to see the exoskeleton.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:53.780
Then as we start to go up in magnification,
the details of the exoskeleton become clearer.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:57.440
It's cool to see more and more of those details
as we zoom in.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:04.250
And especially when we get to the 100x objective,
which gives us 1000x magnification, you can

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:14.520
make out so much of the insides of the Tetrahymena,
that they look like bags of very resilient bubbles.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:23.780
Here’s another ciliate called a Pseudoprorodon
that James looked at through all of these objectives.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:28.060
At first it just looks like a little green
thing floating around and knocking into stuff.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:33.690
But as we go up in magnification, we can start
to make out the subtle undulations of its

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:37.669
body, which shifts the different shades of
green.

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:43.610
And as we look even closer, the green itself
takes on a new life, revealing the perimeters

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:48.290
of the individual algae inside the pseudoprorodon.

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:53.880
Revealing those details inside the tetrahymena
and pseudoprorodon comes at a cost.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:58.080
We can’t see as much of the world around
them, whether that’s the emptiness around

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:04.590
the rotifer exoskeleton or the objects that
the pseudoprorodon keeps bumping into.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:09.419
Whether or not you need those details depends
on what you’re trying to learn or what we

00:06:09.419 --> 00:06:14.830
are trying to show, and that’s why having
the flexibility to look through a wider range

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:19.330
of objectives is such an exciting upgrade
for us.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:25.790
Objectives are our gateways to the microcosmos,
and we’re excited to have all of these new

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.100
scales to look at.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:34.889
We’ve also made some other upgrades so we
can revisit techniques we used on our older microscopes.

00:06:34.889 --> 00:06:40.000
Like a polarizer, because who doesn’t love
this shiny, iridescent stuff?

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.520
But we’re not just sticking with what we’ve
known.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:49.819
Thanks to this upgrade, we’ve got a whole
new way to look at cells: fluorescence microscopy.

00:06:49.819 --> 00:06:54.120
And we are so excited for this one.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.660
The techniques we’ve highlighted so far
are all clever manipulations of white light,

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:01.470
a mixture of many visible wavelengths of light.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:07.570
But in fluorescence microscopy, instead of
illuminating our sample with that mixture,

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:11.240
we choose a specific wavelength of light.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:17.180
That light is called the excitation light
because, well, it excites certain structures

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:19.130
based on their chemistry.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:23.700
And those excitable structures then emit some
light of their own.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:28.790
The emission light will then travel back through
the objective, get past barriers that filter

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:34.610
out the excitation light, and then show up
in our eyes as vivid color.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:39.840
With our upgrade, we have four different wavelengths
of light that we can use for fluorescence

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:47.490
microscopy, and the parts of the sample that
we see fluoresce will depend on which light we use.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:52.830
In this footage, we’re looking at the Pseudoprorodon
that we saw earlier, only this time it’s

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:56.479
being hit with 385 nanometer light.

00:07:56.479 --> 00:08:02.660
The red we see is the fluorescence from natural
structures, this is called autofluorescence.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:09.530
The rest of the organism is dark because it
did not respond to that 385 nanometer light,

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:12.350
making the color all the more vibrant.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:18.120
But the power of fluorescence microscopy is
not just finding fluorescent structures that

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:19.509
exist in nature.

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:24.310
Chemists and biologists have developed all
sorts of ingenious techniques to highlight

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:29.730
different parts of cells using fluorescent
dyes and proteins.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:34.989
James is working on building up his arsenal
of fluorescence microscopy tools and techniques

00:08:34.989 --> 00:08:41.339
so that soon, we’ll be able to dive even
deeper into the microcosmos to seek out things

00:08:41.339 --> 00:08:49.209
that have been hidden from us before, uncovering
the unseen world inside the unseen world that

00:08:49.209 --> 00:08:55.699
surrounds us
Thank you for coming on this journey with

00:08:55.699 --> 00:09:00.559
us and thank you to Brilliant for sponsoring
this episode.

